    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-q/q8-question-1.svg\" alt=\"It is given that for θ ∈ , ϕ ∈ 3tan θ = 4tan ϕ . Show that the above relationship implies that sin 2 θ tan ( θ − ϕ ) = . ( ) 7 7 + cos2 θ\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-q/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Since tan ϕ = 3 4 tan θ, multiply numerator and denominator in the subtraction formula by four: tan(θ −ϕ) = tan θ −tan ϕ 1 + tan θ tan ϕ = 4 tan θ −4 tan ϕ 4 + 4 tan θ tan ϕ = 4 tan θ −(3 tan θ) 4 + tan θ(3 tan θ) = 4 tan θ −3 tan θ 4 + 3 tan2 θ tan θ = 4 + 3 tan2 θ Multiply through by cos2 θ, then by two, and apply the two identities cos2 θ = (1 + cos 2θ)/2 and sin2 θ = (1 −cos 2θ)/2: sin θ cos θ = 4 cos2 θ + 3 sin2 θ 2 sin θ cos θ\" style=\"--madas-width:624.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-q/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"= 8 cos2 θ + 6 sin2 θ sin 2θ = 8(1 2 + 1 2 cos 2θ) + 6( 1 2 −1 2 cos 2θ) sin 2θ = 4 + 4 cos 2θ + 3 −3 cos 2θ sin 2θ = 7 + cos 2θ This gives tan θ tan(θ −ϕ) = 4 + 3 tan2 θ sin θ cos θ = 4 cos2 θ + 3 sin2 θ sin 2θ = 7 + cos 2θ .\" style=\"--madas-width:452.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-q/q8-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"[M1] Use the tangent subtraction formula [M1] substitute the tangent relation [B1] rewrite in sine and cosine [M1] apply the double-angle identities The subtraction formula denominator is positive on the specified domain: [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:634.7px\"></figure>",
